Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API:

---

## Tool Class (`WeatherTools.cs`)

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current weather for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetWeather(
        [Description("City name (e.g., 'London')")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/data/2.5/weather?q={city}&appid=YOUR_KEY&units=metric",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get 5-day forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        [Description("Number of days (1-5)")] int days = 3,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching {Days}-day forecast for {City}", days, city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/data/2.5/forecast?q={city}&cnt={days * 8}&appid=YOUR_KEY&units=metric",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

---

## Program.cs (stdio transport)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC protocol channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.openweathermap.org");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

### 1. Constructor injection (non-static class) — recommended for services

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public class MyTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<MyTools> logger)
{
    // httpClient and logger are injected when the class is instantiated
}
```

Register services normally in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.). The SDK resolves the class from the DI container each time a tool is called.

### 2. Method parameter injection (static class)

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class MyTools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Do something")]
    public static async Task<string> DoIt(
        [Description("Input value")] string input,
        HttpClient httpClient,           // injected by DI — hidden from schema
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    { ... }
}
```

**Key rule:** Parameters that are DI-registered services, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, or `IProgress<T>` are **automatically injected** and never appear in the tool's JSON schema shown to LLMs. Only parameters with `[Description]` attributes become tool inputs.

---

### Quick start commands

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build && dotnet run
```
